1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,929
Lesson 10.3.

2
00:00:02,309 --> 00:00:03,837
DyT normalization.

3
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:09,559
Layer normalization computes the
mean and variance of each activation

4
00:00:09,559 --> 00:00:13,634
vector: two global reductions
over the whole model dimension.

5
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:16,365
On hardware with local
connectivity, summing

6
00:00:16,365 --> 00:00:18,610
over thousands of components is expensive.

7
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:23,480
In twenty twenty-five, Zhu and
colleagues showed it can be replaced by

8
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:27,907
something much simpler, a scaled
hyperbolic tangent, and that tangent

9
00:00:27,907 --> 00:00:31,499
turns out to be exactly what
a pbit computes on average.

10
00:00:32,617 --> 00:00:38,856
Dynamic tanh, DyT, applies γ
tanh(α x) + β to each component,

11
00:00:38,856 --> 00:00:44,585
with a learned scalar α and
per-channel vectors γ and β.

12
00:00:44,965 --> 00:00:48,508
The motivation is empirical:
in trained transformers, the

13
00:00:48,508 --> 00:00:52,673
relationship between the input and
output of layer normalization is

14
00:00:52,673 --> 00:00:56,465
S-shaped, linear in the center
and saturated at the extremes.

15
00:00:56,845 --> 00:01:00,461
DyT reproduces that shape without
computing statistics, and the

16
00:01:00,461 --> 00:01:03,734
authors report comparable
quality in vision and language.

17
00:01:04,850 --> 00:01:08,740
Recall the pbit: with input
u, it outputs plus one

18
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:12,474
with probability σ(2βu)
and minus one otherwise.

19
00:01:12,854 --> 00:01:20,227
Its mean output is 2σ(2βu)
− 1 = tanh(βu), exactly.

20
00:01:20,607 --> 00:01:25,558
A pbit with gain β = α is,
on average, a DyT unit.

21
00:01:25,938 --> 00:01:28,638
Normalization becomes
a physical phenomenon

22
00:01:28,638 --> 00:01:31,213
of the device, with no global reductions.

23
00:01:32,317 --> 00:01:35,427
But a pbit gives a binary
sample, not the mean.

24
00:01:35,807 --> 00:01:38,492
To estimate the tangent one must average S

25
00:01:38,492 --> 00:01:41,562
samples, and the error
falls as one over root S.

26
00:01:41,942 --> 00:01:47,521
We measure it: with one sample,
the root-mean-square error is

27
00:01:47,521 --> 00:01:52,918
0.54; with sixteen, 0.14; with
two hundred fifty-six, 0.03.

28
00:01:53,298 --> 00:01:56,496
Each factor of four in
samples halves the error.

29
00:01:57,617 --> 00:02:01,144
Here a design question appears,
whether we need to average.

30
00:02:01,524 --> 00:02:07,082
If the next layer is also stochastic and
sums many inputs, the noise of each sample

31
00:02:07,082 --> 00:02:11,971
averages out naturally in that sum,
as in the pbit networks of unit four.

32
00:02:12,351 --> 00:02:15,426
Often a few samples, or
even one, suffice, as

33
00:02:15,426 --> 00:02:18,160
long as training accounts for the noise.

34
00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:23,793
Lesson 10.5 quantifies how many effective
bits each number of samples gives.

35
00:02:24,917 --> 00:02:25,727
Let's compare.

36
00:02:26,107 --> 00:02:30,655
Layer normalization needs, per
token, two reductions over the

37
00:02:30,655 --> 00:02:34,532
whole model dimension, a
square root and a division.

38
00:02:34,912 --> 00:02:38,853
DyT needs, per component, one
multiplication, one tangent and

39
00:02:38,853 --> 00:02:42,407
one addition, with no
communication between components.

40
00:02:42,787 --> 00:02:45,836
On a TSU, the tangent is
done by the pbit itself.

41
00:02:46,216 --> 00:02:49,738
That is why language models
for probabilistic chips,

42
00:02:49,738 --> 00:02:53,261
such as Z1T, use DyT instead
of layer normalization.

43
00:02:54,383 --> 00:02:58,538
In practice, replace normalization
with DyT and tune the

44
00:02:58,538 --> 00:03:02,915
initial value of α; map each
unit to a pbit with gain equal

45
00:03:02,915 --> 00:03:06,550
to α; and train with the
sampling noise included.

46
00:03:06,930 --> 00:03:10,381
In lab twenty-nine you will
do it on a small transformer.

47
00:03:11,483 --> 00:03:15,713
Common mistakes: believing DyT
normalizes, when it only bounds.

48
00:03:16,093 --> 00:03:20,388
Confusing the pbit's sigmoid,
whose argument has a factor of two.

49
00:03:20,768 --> 00:03:24,368
Averaging few samples without
including the noise in training.

50
00:03:24,748 --> 00:03:28,541
And forgetting that α is learned
and sensitive to initialization.

51
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:33,718
In summary: DyT replaces
layer normalization with

52
00:03:33,718 --> 00:03:37,288
γ tanh(α x) + β, with
no global reductions.

53
00:03:37,668 --> 00:03:40,976
The mean output of a pbit is exactly a

54
00:03:40,976 --> 00:03:44,981
hyperbolic tangent, so
DyT is native to a TSU.

55
00:03:45,361 --> 00:03:48,833
The price is sampling
noise, which falls as one

56
00:03:48,833 --> 00:03:51,936
over root S and which training can absorb.

57
00:03:53,050 --> 00:03:57,449
To practice: prove the identity
between the sigmoid and the tangent.

58
00:03:57,829 --> 00:04:00,313
Compute the variance of a pbit sample.

59
00:04:00,693 --> 00:04:03,996
Compute the samples for an error of 0.05.

60
00:04:04,376 --> 00:04:08,934
And explain why DyT is cheaper than
layer normalization on local hardware.
